          </h2>

          <p>
            Estrogen does more than regulate the menstrual cycle. It helps maintain bone by slowing the cells that break bone down, and it appears to support muscle mass and the way muscle responds to exercise. As estrogen falls during perimenopause and after the final period, both tissues lose some of that protection.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bone shows it most clearly. In the long-running Study of Women's Health Across the Nation, known as SWAN, bone density at the spine fell by roughly 2-2.5 percent a year during the fastest phase of loss, which began about a year before the final period and continued for a few years after. Loss slows after that but does not stop. The Bone Health and Osteoporosis Foundation estimates that about one in two women over 50 will break a bone because of osteoporosis.
          </p>

          <p>
            Muscle loss is slower and less dramatic, but it adds up. Adults typically lose muscle mass and strength gradually from midlife, and studies suggest the menopause transition is associated with an acceleration of that decline in women, along with a shift toward more abdominal fat. Less muscle means a lower resting metabolism, weaker legs and a higher risk of falls, which matters because falls are what turn fragile bones into fractures.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two problems are linked, and so are the solutions. Muscle pulling on bone during exercise is one of the main signals that keeps bone strong. Resistance training is the single most effective lifestyle tool for both, and protein is what lets muscle respond to it. In the LIFTMOR trial, supervised high-intensity resistance and impact training improved bone density in postmenopausal women with low bone mass, showing that the skeleton still responds to load at this age.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE PRIORITY ORDER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Lift first, eat enough protein second, supplement third.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Strength training two or three times a week does most of the work. Getting 25-30 g of protein at each meal lets your muscles respond. A protein powder is simply a convenient way to hit that per-meal number, especially at breakfast.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-much" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Much Protein Do Women Need During and After Menopause?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The US RDA for adult women is 0.8 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day, about 46 g for a reference woman, and it does not change with menopause. But the RDA was set to prevent deficiency in healthy adults, not to protect muscle in an aging body, and many experts now argue it is too low for older adults.
          </p>

          <p>
            The PROT-AGE study group and the European Society for Clinical Nutrition and Metabolism recommend 1.0-1.2 g per kilogram a day for healthy adults over 65, and more for those who are active, ill or losing muscle. Those recommendations were written for older adults generally rather than for menopause specifically, but many dietitians apply a similar range from the menopause transition onward, especially for women who strength train. For a 65 kg woman, 1.0-1.2 g per kilogram is roughly 65-80 g a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Total protein matters, and so does timing. Many women eat little protein at breakfast, a moderate amount at lunch and most of it at dinner. Studies in older adults suggest that spreading protein more evenly, with a meaningful dose at each meal, is a better way to keep muscle-building signals active through the day. A protein shake is one of the easiest ways to fix a weak breakfast.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have kidney disease, a higher protein intake may not be appropriate, so talk to your doctor before increasing it. For everyone else, intakes in this range are considered safe. Our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> will give you a personal daily target, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-seniors/">best protein powder for seniors</a> guide covers the higher needs and different priorities of women over 70.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="leucine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Leucine per Meal: The Number That Matters Most
          </h2>

          <p>
            Leucine is an essential amino acid that acts as a trigger for muscle protein synthesis, the process that builds and repairs muscle. Aging muscle becomes less sensitive to protein, a phenomenon researchers call anabolic resistance, so older adults need a larger dose per meal to get the same response a younger person gets from less.
          </p>

          <p>
            Research by Moore and colleagues estimated that older adults need about 0.4 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per meal to maximize the muscle-building response, compared with about 0.24 g per kilogram in younger adults. For a 65 kg woman, that is roughly 26 g of high-quality protein per meal. Translated into leucine, many researchers use a target of about 2.5-3 g per meal.
          </p>

