            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can protein powder help prevent osteoporosis?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Protein supports bone, and research shows higher protein intakes are not harmful and may help slightly when calcium is adequate. But protein alone does not prevent osteoporosis. Strength and impact training, enough calcium and vitamin D, avoiding smoking and getting a bone density test when recommended matter more. Some women also need prescription treatment.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does protein powder help with menopause weight gain?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can help indirectly. Protein is filling, helps preserve muscle while you eat a little less, and a lean whey isolate adds protein with few calories. Combined with strength training, that helps limit the shift from muscle to fat common in midlife. A shake does not cause weight loss on its own; total calories and activity still decide the outcome.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is collagen good for menopause?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Collagen is not a good primary protein for muscle because it is low in leucine and lacks tryptophan. A few small studies suggest specific collagen peptides may modestly affect bone markers or skin, but the evidence is limited. If you enjoy it, use collagen as an extra on top of a complete protein such as whey, casein, soy or pea.
                </p>
